Based on Lemma \ref{lemma-episolcol} and the fact that $0 < \beta \le \tfrac{1}{28}$,  we conclude that $\left(x^{k+1}, y^{k+1}\right)$ is an $\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{1/{K\beta}})$-GS of problem \eqref{eq:problem}. Moreover, by using the same argument as in \eqref{eq:os-gs}, we can show that $z^{k+1}$ is an $\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{1/{K\beta}})$-OS of problem \eqref{eq:problem}. Upon setting $\beta = C K^{-\frac{1}{2}}$ for the concave case and  $\beta = C K^{-\frac{2\theta-1}{2\theta}}$ for the case where the K{\L} exponent $\theta \in (\tfrac{1}{2}, 1)$, with $C$ being a suitably chosen constant, we see that the 
